Java is a typed language which means to define a variable we need to define which type of data we need to store.
For eg, int, char, string, float etc.
    Data Types in Java:-
a. Primitive :- byte, short, char, boolean, int, long, float, double  
b. Non-Primitive :- String, Array, Class, Object, interface
Call by Value :- Passing an copy of original variable. Java always call by value.
  -> When you pass a variable to a function, it is a copy of a original variable that is created inside a stack frame 
      of that function. If you return to the main function stack frame of the function will be deleted and all changes to 
      that passing variable also gets deleted.
Call by Reference :- Those function in which we pass an original value. In C++ we pass original copy.
Function Overloading :- Multiple functions with the same name but different parameters.
  Note:- Function Overloading does not depend on the return type of the function, it will treat same if the name is same.
          eg, int sum(int a, int b)  &   float sum(int a, int b)  - Here both are same , so it's is not function overloading
                it will give error when compile.
